    "result_text": "The change is complete and the test suite is green. Here's a summary of what I implemented.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe `sharesHousehold` opt-in already existed (it skipped the duplicate-household rejection), but no shared `householdId` was ever assigned or returned. I closed that gap:\n\n**Contract (`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`)** \u2014 Added a read-only `householdId` string property to the `Owner` schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` exposes it and it's returned by `GET /api/owners/{id}`.\n\n**Entity (`model/Owner.java`)** \u2014 Added a `householdId` field (`@Column(name = \"household_id\")`) with getter/setter, so it persists and MapStruct maps it onto the DTO automatically.\n\n**Persistence (all four `db/*/schema.sql`)** \u2014 Added a `household_id` column to the `owners` table (hsqldb \u2014 used by the tests \u2014 plus h2, mysql, postgres for parity).\n\n**Controller (`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`)** \u2014 When `sharesHousehold` is true, it now skips the duplicate check *and* assigns a `householdId` before saving.\n\n**Stable shared identifier** \u2014 The new `householdId(lastName, address)` helper derives the ID deterministically: the first 16 upper-case hex chars of `SHA-256(canonicalLastName + \"\\n\" + canonicalAddress)`, reusing the existing `canonicalizeHousehold` normalization (trim, collapse whitespace, lower-case). Because it's a pure function of the canonical last name + address, every owner in the same household gets the identical value \u2014 no cross-record lookup needed \u2014 and `\"\\n\"` is a safe delimiter since canonicalization collapses all whitespace, so it can't appear in either field.\n\nThe acceptance test `coreJoinersShareHouseholdId` (owners B and C, same last name + address, both `sharesHousehold: true`) now sees an identical, non-blank `householdId` for both. `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` / `BUILD SUCCESS`.\n\nOne note: per the spec's wording and the OpenAPI description, `householdId` is assigned only when a request opts in with `sharesHousehold: true`; a plain owner (like `a` in the test) has no `householdId`. Since the ID is derived deterministically, any future household joiner still computes the same value regardless.",
